WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.503
Lesson seven.

00:00:01.503 --> 00:00:03.041
Reading the weights.

00:00:03.041 --> 00:00:07.380
Because the pilot is linear, we can understand exactly what it learned.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.218
Let's take it apart.

00:00:09.818 --> 00:00:11.673
Start with one decision.

00:00:11.673 --> 00:00:14.398
The lander is high up and falling fast.

00:00:14.398 --> 00:00:19.558
Each input is multiplied by its weight, and the products are added up per action.

00:00:19.558 --> 00:00:25.181
The falling speed gives the main engine a big push, and takes a lot away from do nothing.

00:00:25.181 --> 00:00:28.090
It's close, but the main engine wins.

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:32.040
A moment later, the lander is tilted to the right.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:36.775
The tilt now adds votes to the left engine and takes them from the others.

00:00:36.775 --> 00:00:40.771
The left engine wins, and turns the lander back upright.

00:00:41.371 --> 00:00:44.560
Further down, the lander is spinning to the left.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:49.650
Spin has the biggest weight of all, on the right engine, and here it counts for it.

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:53.139
The right engine wins, and spins the lander back.

00:00:53.739 --> 00:00:56.348
And on the ground, with both legs down.

00:00:56.348 --> 00:00:59.901
The two leg switches each add a big vote for do nothing.

00:00:59.901 --> 00:01:02.787
Do nothing wins, and the lander stays still.

00:01:02.787 --> 00:01:06.029
That's the rule the settle penalty taught it.

00:01:06.629 --> 00:01:07.831
But be careful.

00:01:07.831 --> 00:01:10.652
A big weight doesn't always mean a big effect.

00:01:10.652 --> 00:01:13.355
Here are the sizes of all the weights.

00:01:13.955 --> 00:01:18.643
What also matters is how much each input actually changes in flight.

00:01:18.643 --> 00:01:20.987
Altitude and drift move a lot.

00:01:20.987 --> 00:01:25.922
Spin moves less, so its large weights are multiplied by smaller numbers.

00:01:25.922 --> 00:01:28.936
The legs are always zero in the air.

00:01:29.536 --> 00:01:33.472
Multiply the two, and you get the influence of each weight.

00:01:33.472 --> 00:01:39.763
The ones that matter most are tilt and spin on the right engine, and drift on the left engine.

00:01:40.363 --> 00:01:42.788
We can also draw a rule as a map.

00:01:42.788 --> 00:01:48.940
Across is the tilt, and up and down is the spin, with every other input held at a typical value.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:52.967
Each colour is the action the pilot would choose there.

00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:55.933
The white line is a real flight.

00:01:55.933 --> 00:02:00.547
The border between the two engines runs diagonally through the middle.

00:02:00.547 --> 00:02:08.182
Tilt or spin one way, and the pilot fires the engine that turns it back, so it stays upright with small corrections.

00:02:08.782 --> 00:02:10.414
Here's a second map.

00:02:10.414 --> 00:02:14.620
Across is the height, and up and down is the falling speed.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:18.376
Falling fast and low, it fires the main engine.

00:02:18.376 --> 00:02:22.049
Falling slowly, it steers with the side engines instead.

00:02:22.049 --> 00:02:24.460
And high up, it doesn't brake yet.

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.928
It saves the main engine for later.

00:02:26.928 --> 00:02:30.958
The real flight rides along that border all the way down.

00:02:31.558 --> 00:02:34.739
Now let's put it together, one action at a time.

00:02:34.739 --> 00:02:36.841
The main engine is the brake.

00:02:36.841 --> 00:02:43.561
Falling speed has the biggest weight on it, so the faster the lander falls, the harder it wants to brake.

00:02:44.161 --> 00:02:46.493
Do nothing is the landing rule.

00:02:46.493 --> 00:02:50.321
Each leg that touches the ground adds a big vote for it.

00:02:50.321 --> 00:02:54.388
With both legs down, it wins, and the engines go quiet.

00:02:54.988 --> 00:02:57.693
The left engine fixes drift and tilt.

00:02:57.693 --> 00:03:03.826
It fires when the lander drifts right, sits right of the pad, tilts right, or spins clockwise.

00:03:03.826 --> 00:03:07.252
It pushes the lander back toward the middle.

00:03:07.852 --> 00:03:10.681
And the right engine is its mirror image.

00:03:10.681 --> 00:03:16.282
Drifting left, tilting left, or spinning the other way, and the right engine pushes back.

00:03:16.282 --> 00:03:19.400
Nobody told evolution the lander is symmetric.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:21.605
It found that on its own.

00:03:22.205 --> 00:03:25.976
Finally, let's test which weights the pilot really needs.

00:03:25.976 --> 00:03:30.850
We set one weight to zero, fly thirty episodes, and see how many still land.

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:34.051
Then we put it back, and try the next one.

00:03:34.651 --> 00:03:37.188
Most weights can go with little harm.

00:03:37.188 --> 00:03:42.994
But remove the rule that links falling speed to the main engine, and not a single flight lands.

00:03:42.994 --> 00:03:46.432
Without it, the pilot never brakes, and simply falls.

00:03:46.432 --> 00:03:48.912
The leg rules matter almost as much.

00:03:48.912 --> 00:03:52.087
Remove one of them, and most landings fail.
